# Milestone: M3
# Covers: a tag stop delivered at an internal safepoint inside a `|`-chain
#         unwinds the current strand through the cleanup stack, executing
#         finally bodies and popping TAG_SCOPE entries in order (row 12 §5.3
#         — safepoint delivery of pending UEXEC_TAG_STOP unwind).
#
# blocked: parse — `t: { try { ... } finally { ... } }` does not parse; a
#          try/finally is not accepted where the tag-scope body wants a
#          statement
# matrix-row: tag-stop
#
# Reason (re-probed on the re-founded core):
#   The blocker this fixture used to name — "method call syntax requires
#   the M4 object model" — has been false for a long time: `t.stop()`
#   resolves and runs.  What actually stops it is the parse.  A bare
#   `t: { try { 1 } finally { 2 } }` is accepted, but the body below is
#   not; the parser rejects it at the `finally`.  Whether that is the
#   `|`-chain inside the try or the try-as-tag-scope-body shape is the
#   thing to establish before rewriting, and it is a frontend question
#   rather than a fixture one.
#
# The body, once it parses:
#   var log = ""
#   var t = Tag.new()
#   t: {
#     try { log = log + "a" | t.stop() | log = log + "c" }
#     finally { log = log + "F" }
#   }
#   log
#   --- expected output ---
#   [00000001] "aF"
